{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-12-110-spring-2007", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["12.110"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Sedimentary Geology", "course_slug": "12-110-sedimentary-geology-spring-2007", "term": "Spring", "year": 2007, "as_taught_in": "Spring 2007", "language": "en", "departments": ["Earth, Atmospheric, and Planetary Sciences"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Prof. John Southard"], "subject_primary": "Earth Science", "subject_secondary": ["Geochemistry", "Geology", "Geophysics", "Oceanography"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Earth Science", "specialties": ["Geochemistry", "Geology", "Geophysics", "Oceanography"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Activity Assignments with Examples", "Lecture Notes"], "description": "Survey of the important aspects of modern sediments and ancient sedimentary rocks. Emphasis is on fundamental materials, features, and processes. Textures of siliciclastic sediments and sedimentary rocks: particle size, particle shape, and particle packing. Mechanics of sediment transport. Survey of siliciclastic sedimentary rocks: sandstones, conglomerates, and shales. Carbonate sediments and sedimentary rocks; cherts; evaporites. Siliciclastic and carbonate diagenesis. Paleontology, with special reference to fossils in sedimentary rocks. Modern and ancient depositional environments. Stratigraphy. Sedimentary basins. Fossil fuels: coal, petroleum.", "course_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-110-sedimentary-geology-spring-2007/", "image_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-110-sedimentary-geology-spring-2007/1b9123d7217a15f66f33dbe579ac1f52_12-110s07.jpg", "license_name": "CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/", "resources": {"syllabus": true, "calendar": false